---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SampleSize from '@/components/tools/calc2/SampleSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sample-size-calculator">
  <SampleSize client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the sample size calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The sample size calculator finds how many responses give a chosen margin of error at a chosen
      confidence level, for a survey that measures a proportion.
    </p>
    <pre><code>n₀ = z² × p(1 − p) ÷ E²
n  = n₀ ÷ (1 + (n₀ − 1) ÷ N)     with a population of N
round n up to a whole response</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      For 95% confidence (z = 1.95996), a margin of ±5% (E = 0.05) and p = 0.5:
      n₀ = 1.95996² × 0.25 ÷ 0.0025 = 384.15, so 385 responses. From a population of 1,000 people,
      n = 384.15 ÷ (1 + 383.15 ÷ 1,000) = 277.7, so 278.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the sample grows so fast</h2>
    <p>
      The margin of error falls with the square root of n. To halve the margin from ±5% to ±2.5% you need
      four times the responses: 1,537 instead of 385. The curve in the calculator shows this.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The formula assumes a simple random sample. Clustered or weighted samples need a design effect.</li>
      <li>The result is the number of completed responses. Divide by the expected response rate to find how many people to ask.</li>
      <li>For means, use n = (z σ ÷ E)² with an estimate of the standard deviation σ.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
